From: Matthew Dowell <mbd06b+github@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 09:38:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/12] fix: rev-parse reads its arguments in Git's order; -C
 fails like Git

Review FIX FIRST items A1, A3 and A4, plus the rev-parse parity cases
that fall out of reading arguments the way Git does.

- `rev-parse` now reads flags, path queries and revisions in one ordered
  pass modelled on Git's argv loop. `--verify`/`--short` stop files and
  `--` from reaching stdout, so `--verify HEAD -- x` prints one object
  name; `HEAD --` keeps the delimiter; `missing -- x` is a bad revision
  with empty stdout. Flags apply from their position (`HEAD --short HEAD`),
  `--verify ^HEAD` prints `^<oid>`, `--verify A..B` prints the range and
  fails as Git 2.52 does, `A...B` shows the merge bases, `--short=` and
  `--short=-1` use the 4-digit minimum, repeated `--git-dir` prints each
  time, and `HEAD:missing` / `:missing` name the path like Git.
- Failures return a typed `Exit` instead of calling `process::exit`, so
  global `-v` forwards buffered stdout and stderr before the process ends;
  brit-cli maps `Exit` to its status without an `Error:` report.
- Inside a `.git` directory discovery no longer looks for `./.git/objects`
  (`discovery_start`), and `--show-toplevel` fails with Git's
  "must be run in a work tree" and 128.
- `-C <missing>` prints `fatal: cannot change to '<dir>': <strerror>` and
  exits 128, also before a subcommand's `--help` or usage error.
- The module docs list the remaining known differences and no longer
  claim Git's messages for every failure; the `-C` help no longer says
  "before anything else".

